{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1548384349,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE3NjEwMzY2Nw=="},"target":"a31c7525a2047a642af667e544577c66e39ed35950d6e719358dfbb77803f6da","message":"ignorant me thought that annex export happens automagically upon \"annex copy\" if remote is configured with `exporttree=yes`. [Apparently](https://git-annex.branchable.com/git-annex-export/) and unfortunately it is not the case and it requires use of dedicated `annex export BRANCH` command to export the tree to such special remote.  I think we could/should simplify publishing to the \"exported\" special remotes by calling `annex export` (not copy) if the annex special remote has exporttree configured.  The possible gotcha (I didn't work with exported remotes much) is confusion if export is attempted from different branches.\n\nBTW -- here is a sample code to produce/export to such  special rsync remote:\n```shell\ndatalad create /tmp/testds; cd /tmp/testds; echo 123 \u003e 123; datalad add 123 \u0026\u0026 \\\n  git annex initremote rsync-remote type=rsync rsyncurl=localhost:/tmp/testds-rsync encryption=none exporttree=yes \u0026\u0026 \\\n  git annex export --to=rsync-remote master \u0026\u0026 \\\n  ls -l /tmp/testds-rsync \n```","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1548384131,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDIwOTYxNjY4NDU="},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]}]}